-- Passage rg8-context-clues-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('dea1a239-7b14-599e-9914-0ddbc58f17f7', 'rg8-context-clues-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Keepers of the Light$q$, $q$For more than two centuries, lighthouses guided ships along rocky coastlines. Each one needed a keeper, a person whose job was to make sure the light never went out. The work demanded that keepers stay vigilant through the night. They watched the flame, trimmed the wick, and checked the weather, because a single careless hour could put a ship in danger.

The waters near many lighthouses were treacherous. Hidden rocks, shifting sandbars, and sudden fog could tear open the hull of a ship that strayed even slightly off course. A strong beacon, a bright light that sailors could see from miles away, was often the only warning they had.

Life at a lighthouse could be lonely. Some keepers lived on tiny islands with no neighbors at all, and supply boats might arrive only once a month. To pass the long evenings, many keepers read, carved wood, or kept detailed journals about the ships and storms they saw.

Keepers also had to be frugal. Oil for the lamp was expensive, so they used it carefully and wasted nothing. Spare parts were hard to get, so a broken tool was repaired rather than replaced.

In the twentieth century, automatic lights and electronic navigation made the keeper's job obsolete. Machines could now do the work that once required a person, and the last keepers gradually left their posts. Today, many old lighthouses have become museums, where visitors can climb the winding stairs and imagine a night spent guarding the light.$q$, 'draft')
