-- Passage rg8-context-clues-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c77c3fe8-a781-573b-8959-df93d4610cf9', 'rg8-context-clues-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Saturday at the Bakery$q$, $q$Hiroshi was reluctant to work at his uncle's bakery over spring break. He had hoped to sleep late and play video games, and he dragged his feet all the way to the shop at five in the morning.

"You are a novice, so we will start simple," Uncle Kenji said. "Nobody expects a beginner to shape bread on the first day." He handed Hiroshi a stack of flat paper boxes to fold.

Folding boxes turned out to be tedious. Each one took the same six folds, and after the fortieth box Hiroshi's fingers ached and his mind had gone numb with boredom.

At six, Uncle Kenji showed him how to proof the dough. "To proof it means to let it rest in a warm place so it can rise," he explained, sliding a tray into a cabinet that felt like a summer afternoon. "If you skip this step, the loaves come out flat and heavy."

By seven, customers were lining up outside. The shelves that had been full an hour earlier were almost bare, and only a few rolls remained in the basket. Uncle Kenji pointed at the empty racks. "Time to replenish," he said, and together they carried out fresh trays until every shelf was full again.

When the morning rush was over, a woman with a stroller stopped at the counter to thank them. "Your cinnamon bread is the best part of my week," she said. Hiroshi noticed that he was smiling. Five in the morning, he decided, was not quite as terrible as he had expected.$q$, 'draft')
